When I was laid off in June 2025, I expected a rough transition. What I didn’t expect was that applying for unemployment insurance in Virginia would become the hardest user journey <br> I’ve ever navigated—and I work in systems design for a living. <br><br>

I don’t consider myself an inflammatory person. I’m someone who gives the benefit of the doubt. When a service fails, I tend to assume it’s a bad day, not a bad system. But the <br> Virginia Employment Commission (VEC) tested that philosophy to its limits. <br><br>

It began with a login issue. Then came broken authentication, rejected uploads, unsaveable forms, inaccessible buttons, and entire pages that switched languages without warning.  I <br> encountered dark pattern after dark pattern, each one a roadblock demanding workarounds.  Some days, I translated the site with ChatGPT to make sense of it. Other days, I <br> physically drove to the Career Works office — where I learned from kind and overworked staff that even the phone system has undocumented rules you have to memorize if you don’t want <br> your claim delayed.<br><br>

Each visit felt like uncovering a hidden level of a poorly designed game. One that punishes you for playing. And still, after a full month, I have received no payment. <br><br>

Somewhere in the middle of it, a thought hit me like a punchline: *Maybe this system wasn’t broken by accident. Maybe this is what it looks like when a system works exactly as <br> designed.* <br><br>

It was a bitter realization. Every digital interface is made of decisions. People built this system. Others funded it. And someone, somewhere, allowed it to remain this hard to use <br> for years—during one of the most financially vulnerable moments in a person’s life. <br><br>

And that’s what stayed with me. Not the bugs. Not even the lack of support. But the very human cost of a system that quietly exhausts people into giving up. I didn’t give up, but I <br> easily could have. <br><br>

This experience didn’t just reaffirm why I do user-centered work—it sharpened my sense of responsibility as a designer and researcher. Good systems serve people when they’re at their <br> worst, not just their best. They lower friction. They preserve dignity. They don’t bury you under bureaucracy when you’re already buried under stress. <br><br>

The real question isn’t “why doesn’t this work?” <br>
It’s: *Who benefits when it doesn’t?*
